  "id": "Compound::DB01036 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Tolterodine, a cholinergic muscarinic antagonist, is linked to Chrm3 through a series of molecular interactions that collectively support its potential to modulate muscarinic receptor activity. The evidence suggests that Tolterodine, as part of the cholinergic muscarinic antagonist class, binds to Chrm3 through a shared mechanism with other antimuscarinic agents such as Darifenacin, Atropine, and Oxybutynin. These agents all fall under the broader category of organonitrogen compounds and are known to inhibit muscarinic receptors, including Chrm3, by blocking the binding of acetylcholine to the receptor. The lowest common ancestors of Tolterodine and its counterparts—Antimuscarinic Agent—highlight a common biological function, reinforcing the notion that these drugs act through a shared pathway involving muscarinic receptor inhibition.\n\nThe integrated evidence also reveals that Tolterodine's interaction with Chrm3 is not unique to a single compound but is part of a broader class of antimuscarinic agents that share a common mechanism of action. This suggests that the therapeutic effect of Tolterodine may be mediated through the inhibition of muscarinic receptors, particularly Chrm3, which is involved in various physiological processes, including smooth muscle contraction and neurotransmitter regulation. The presence of multiple overlapping pathways and the consistent association with muscarinic receptor inhibition support the hypothesis that Tolterodine exerts its effects through a well-defined, mechanistically sound mechanism.",
